Since $\mathbf{A}$ is a permutation matrix composed of a 90-degree rotation, each applied successive multiplication will give the next power of $\mathbf{A}.$

Therefore, multiplying three times $\mathbf{A}^{100}$ is simply equivalent to three rotations.

\[\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} = \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}}.\]. The answer is \begin{pmatrix}0&0&1\1&0&0\0&1&0\end{pmatrix}

\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^2 &= \mathbf{A}\cdot \mathbf{A}
    = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} 
        \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}
        = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix} 
    = -\mathbf{A},
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^4 &= (-\mathbf{A})^2
    = -\mathbf{A}^2
    = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}
    =\mathbf{A}.
\end{align*}
Hence, $\mathbf{A}^{100} = \mathbf{A}^4 \cdot \mathbf{A}^8 \cdot \mathbf{A}^{24} \cdot \mathbf{A}^{20} = \mathbf{A}^2 \cdot \mathbf{A} = - \mathbf{A},$ so $\mathbf{A}^{100} = \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & -1 \\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & -1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}}.$[asy]
